in the friedel-crafts a solution  reaction an aromatic compound is reacted  with an acid chloride in the presence of  aluminum trichloride this adds the acyl  group onto the aromatic ring let's look  at the mechanism of this reaction in the  first step the acid chloride is going to  react with aluminum trichloride  the chlorine will be transferred on to  aluminum in a process similar to  friedel-crafts alkylation you may want  to check out my video on that if you  haven't already so the chlorine is going  to go with its lone pairs onto aluminum  this gives us a negatively charged  aluminum complex and a positively  charged a cilium eye on the 'silly oh my  on also has a resonance form that puts  the positive charge on oxygen this is an  important contributing structure because  we have an octet on oxygen and on carbon  in this form now we can show the double  bond of benzene attacking at the carbon  of our a cilium ion pushing the  electrons on to oxygen now to get to our  final product all that's left is a  deprotonation although you'll sometimes  see this shown as a spontaneous process  with loss of H+ probably the best way to  draw this is to show a chlorine  dissociating from the aluminum complex  deprotonating making the compound  aromatic and producing a molecule of HCl  let's look at resonance in the product  that we just made so that we can better  understand the nature of an isolated  benzene derivative notice that we have  these conjugated double bonds here so we  can push electrons outside of the ring  through the oxygen atom this charge  separated resonance form isn't the best  resonance form for this molecule but it  does contribute to the overall structure  let's continue to push electrons around  the ring now our carbo cation is para to  this group and we can push  electrons once again to show a final  resonance form there are two things that  we should notice first of all all the  resonance forms put positive charges on  the ring this deactivates the ring  toward further electrophilic aromatic  substitution  if this already has positive character  it doesn't really want to attack a  positive electrophile again so this ring  is deactivated toward further reaction  we should also notice that the positive  charge in the resonance forms is  concentrated on the ortho and para  positions this means when it does react  with an electrophile in EA s it will do  so at the meta positions doing the  friedel-crafts isolation reaction solves  a lot of problems that you would have if  you did the friedel-crafts alkylation  reaction but before I talk to you about  that I first need to show you a new  reaction a carbonyl group can actually  be reduced to a ch2 group these methods  are both named in honor of their  discoverers the first is the wolf  Kirchner reaction by treating the  carbonyl compound with hydrazine in the  presence of NaOH we can reduce it all  the way down to a ch2 group the second  way to do this is the Clemmensen  reduction which uses zinc mercury  amalgam in the presence of acid okay now  let's get back to the problems with the  friedel-crafts alkylation reaction and  see why isolation can be used to solve  them the Elco group is a weakly  activating group and stabilizes the  formation of negative charge on the ring  whereas the acyl group has the resonance  we saw above so it deactivates the ring  to further attack recall with the  friedel-crafts alkylation reaction we  saw this substrate reacting to add on  two alkyl groups and we couldn't stop  with just one well because this product  pulls electron density out of the ring  it's not likely to react further so it  really won't want to react twice with  our acyl chloride since the ring  is deactivated further EAS reactions  that are unwanted are less of a problem  remember carbo cation rearrangements  were a problem with friedel-crafts  alkylation even if we wanted to make a  straight chain on here of carbon atoms  we couldn't because of carbo cation  rearrangements the compound would  rearrange to form a more stable  carbocation if instead we isolate the  ring with a three carbon acid chloride  we can get a linear product now we can  apply one of the reductions that we just  learned to make it into the straight  chain alkyl compound so in a couple  steps we can get two straight chain  alkylated compounds that weren't  possible to make with the friedel-crafts  alkylation reaction KP here if you learn  something give me a thumbs up on the way  out and for more chemistry subscribe to  my channel
 

